            <ArticleTitle>Nonatherosclerotic causes of coronary artery narrowing--Part III.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>656-61</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Approximately 5% of patients with acute myocardial infarction do not have atherosclerotic coronary artery disease but have other causes for their luminal narrowing. The third part of this three-part review of nonatherosclerotic causes of coronary narrowing focuses on coronary vasculitis, infectious diseases, Kawasaki's disease, metabolic disorders, metastatic disease, and substance abuse (cocaine).</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
